  "proof": [
    "Let C=m(m-1), with the literal m in {4,...,13}, and B=sqrt(C)+1. Then g(u)=sqrt(C+u) is increasing and nonnegative on [0,B].",
    "B^2-(C+B)=sqrt(C)>0, so g maps [0,B] strictly below B. Starting at a_0=0, monotonicity of g proves a_(n+1)>=a_n by induction, while interval invariance proves boundedness.",
    "The sequence therefore converges to L. Since a_2>sqrt(C), its limit exceeds sqrt(C). Every later iterate is at most g(B)<B, so L<B.",
    "The inequalities (m-1)^2<C<m^2 imply sqrt(C)<m<sqrt(C)+1. This open interval has length one and contains precisely one integer, m. Every displayed alternative is an integer, so the answer follows without solving the quadratic.",
    "For each nonnegative integer option v, v^2<=C certifies it fails the lower bound; (v-1)^2>=C with v>=1 certifies it fails the upper bound. Exact integer comparisons below resolve all forty literal choices."
  ],
